---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import StatTile from "../../components/StatTile.astro";
import Disclosure from "../../components/Disclosure.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Getting 150+ people to actually use a new system · Sahil Gaji"
  description="Configuring Jira for three cross-functional projects at Bosch was the easy part. Adoption was the project."
>
  <Header current="work" />
  <main>
    <p class="eyebrow">Robert Bosch GmbH · Mar–Aug 2024</p>
    <h1>Getting 150+ people to actually use a new system.</h1>

    <div class="metrics">
      <StatTile value="150+" label="managers & team leads trained" />
      <StatTile value="3" label="cross-functional projects standardized" />
      <StatTile value="~ 15%" label="productivity improvement" definition="Measured after standardizing Jira workflows across the three projects." />
      <StatTile value="~ 30%" label="faster document retrieval" definition="After migrating project documentation from Excel and internal Wiki to Confluence." />
    </div>

    <section class="prose">
      <p>
        Six months as an intern on Bosch's Project Management Digitalization
        team, supporting global supply chain digitalization work across
        international teams. The problem wasn't a lack of tools — it was too
        many of them, used inconsistently: OPLs, roadmaps, and risk tracking
        scattered across spreadsheets and whatever format each project lead
        preferred.
      </p>
      <p>
        Standardizing that onto Jira, for three cross-functional projects,
        took a few weeks. That was the easy part.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="inversion">Configuring the tool was the easy fifth of the work.</p>

    <h2>What the other four-fifths looked like</h2>
    <section class="prose">
      <p>
        Once Jira was configured, the actual work was getting it to stick:
        training sessions, documentation people would come back to, and a
        lot of one-on-one troubleshooting for managers who'd been running
        things their own way for years. The Confluence migration was the
        same problem from a different angle — moving documentation off
        Excel and an internal Wiki only pays off once people trust the new
        place enough to stop keeping their own copies. Once it stuck,
        retrieval time dropped by roughly 30%.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ticket handling was the one piece that ran more in the background:
        an AI chatbot and Power Automate took over the repetitive triage
        that used to eat someone's morning, and a Power BI dashboard put
        ticket-resolution KPIs in front of the team without anyone
        compiling a report by hand.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Disclosure label="Why training mattered more than the tool">
      <p>
        Nobody adopts a new system because it's technically better than the
        old one. They adopt it because someone showed them, specifically,
        how it makes their week easier — and was still around to answer
        questions three weeks later when they forgot how. That's most of
        where six months actually went.
      </p>
    </Disclosure>

    <Disclosure label="What I owned vs. what I supported">
      <p>
        <strong>Owned:</strong> the Jira workflow design for OPLs, roadmaps,
        and risk tracking; the training materials and sessions; the
        Confluence migration; the Power BI dashboard.
        <strong>Supported:</strong> the broader digitalization strategy and
        which tools got chosen in the first place — that direction came from
        the team I was on, not from me as an intern six months in.
      </p>
    </Disclosure>

    <h2>What I'd do differently</h2>
    <div class="retro">
      <p>
        Looking back, I would have asked for feedback earlier and more
        frequently from the people using the tools, rather than assuming the
        first version was already intuitive. Building a good workflow is
        only half the job; adoption depends on understanding how different
        people actually work. That experience reinforced that digital
        transformation is as much about people as it is about technology.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Verify this</h2>
    <ul class="verify">
      <li>This internship is listed on my CV, with the same dates and metrics.</li>
      <li>A reference from this period is available on request, as part of a normal hiring process.</li>
      <li>Screenshots of the Jira workflows, Confluence space, and dashboard aren't included here — they're Bosch's internal systems, not mine to publish.</li>
    </ul>
  </main>
  <Footer lang="en" />
</Base>

<style>
  main {
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-3);
  }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
  }
  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 4.5vw, 2.3rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    text-wrap: balance;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-6);
  }
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    margin: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-3);
  }
  .metrics {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(11rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
  }
  .prose p {
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    max-width: 42rem;
  }
  .inversion {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
    max-width: 38rem;
  }
  .retro {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-line);
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    max-width: 42rem;
  }
  .retro p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .verify {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    max-width: 42rem;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .verify li {
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
</style>
